'''Lant Davan''', designated "'''DC1'''," was a [[human]] [[Gender|male]] [[New Republic soldier|soldier]] and [[corrections officer]] who held the [[Military rank|rank]] of [[lieutenant]] within the [[New Republic Correctional Corps]]. He served as the sole organic crew member on the [[New Republic Correctional Transport|correctional transport]] ''[[Bothan-5]]''. In [[9 ABY]], Davan was present in the [[transport]]'s control room when a [[Ranzar Malk's crew|team]] of [[Mercenary|mercenaries]] [[Bothan-Five incident|infiltrated]] it to free a [[Qin|prisoner]].

During a heated confrontation, Davan pointed his [[A-180 blaster|blaster]] at the criminals and held up a [[Distress beacon|tracking beacon]], threatening to alert the [[New Republic]] of their infiltration. He was [[Death|killed]] when the mercenary [[Xi'an]] threw a [[dagger]] at him, despite the [[Mandalorian]] [[Din Djarin]]'s efforts to prevent any killing, but not before the [[soldier]] activated the beacon. Most of the mercenaries ended up trapped aboard ''Bothan-5'' during their infiltration, with the team leader [[Migs Mayfeld]] receiving a fifty-[[Standard year|year]] sentence at the [[Karthon Chop Fields]] for his involvement in the mission and Davan's death.

==Biography==
===Killed in action===
{{Quote|Stop! Just stop right there.|Davan is alarmed by the mercenaries|audio=Davan-Stop.flac|<ref name="Chapter 6" />}}
In [[9 ABY]],<ref name="Date" /> Lant Davan<ref name="Handbook">''[[Star Wars: The Mandalorian Handbook]]''</ref>&mdash;using the designation "DC1"&mdash;served as a [[New Republic soldier|soldier]]<ref name="Chapter 6" /> and [[corrections officer]]<ref name="Junior novel" /> in the [[New Republic Correctional Corps]], holding the [[Military rank|rank]] of [[lieutenant]].<ref name="Chapter 10" /> He<ref name="Junior novel" /> was the sole organic crew member<ref name="Chapter 6" /> and [[prison]] guard of<ref name="Companion">{{InsiderCite|201|Star Wars: The Mandalorian Season One Companion|''Star Wars: The Mandalorian'' Season One Companion}}</ref> ''[[Bothan-5]]'',<ref name="Handbook" /> one of the [[New Republic]]'s most secure [[New Republic Correctional Transport|correctional transports]],<ref name="Qin DB">{{Databank|qin|Qin}}</ref> which was additionally guarded by New Republic [[security droid]]s. At some point, the [[transport]] was [[Bothan-Five incident|boarded]]<ref name="Chapter 6" /> near the [[Dilestri system]]<ref name="Chapter 14">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 14: The Tragedy}}</ref> by a [[Ranzar Malk's crew|team]] of [[Mercenary|mercenaries]] who sought to rescue the [[prisoner]] [[Qin]]. After defeating a number of New Republic [[N5 sentry droid]]s, they arrived at the control room where Davan was based. The [[soldier]] sprang up from his seat and aimed his [[A-180 blaster]] at the four mercenaries. Despite telling the infiltrators to put down their [[blaster]]s, Davan was slowly surrounded by them.<ref name="Chapter 6" />

[[File:Davan_Dead_The_Mandalorian.png|thumb|left|300px|Davan was killed by Xi'an.]]
Davan held up a [[homing beacon]]&mdash;which would call in New Republic reinforcements&mdash;in an attempt to threaten the mercenaries. The team leader, [[Migs Mayfeld]], told the soldier to put the beacon down, calling him an "egghead." While the leader and two of the other mercenaries wished to [[Death|kill]] the soldier, the [[Mandalorian]] [[bounty hunter]] [[Din Djarin]] opposed the idea, attempting to negotiate with Davan<ref name="Chapter 6" /> as he did not wish to end his life.<ref name="PostGCW">{{EncyclopediaCite|Post-Galactic Civil War and the Mandalorian}}</ref> Mayfeld intervened, insisting on killing the soldier. Djarin argued with Mayfeld and the [[Devaronian]] [[Burg]] to keep Davan alive, igniting a tense standoff between the three while the corrections officer watched, slightly lowering his blaster. The remaining mercenary, the [[Twi'lek]] [[Xi'an]], then threw a [[dagger]] at the soldier, killing him, but not before he activated the beacon. After Davan's murderer removed her dagger from his chest, the mercenaries realized that New Republic reinforcements were on the way<ref name="Chapter 6" /> within the next twenty [[Standard minute|minutes]].<ref name="PostGCW" />

===Legacy===
{{Quote|Security records also show that you put your own life in harm's way to try to protect that of Lieutenant Davan from the New Republic Correctional Corps.|Carson Teva, to Din Djarin|<ref name="Chapter 10" />}}
[[File:MayfeldMugshot.png|thumb|right|250px|Migs Mayfeld was sentenced to fifty years at the Karthon Chop Fields for his association with Davan's death and the breakout of Qin.]]
The mercenaries left the control room and found Qin, and they turned on Djarin and lock him inside a cell. The Mandalorian, however, escaped and returned to the control room, where Davan's corpse still lay. Djarin took Davan's beacon and then trapped the other mercenaries on ''Bothan-5'' before fleeing with Qin. The Mandalorian planted Davan's beacon on the prisoner and delivered him to the [[Roost|space station]] where his [[Ranzar Malk|client]] waited, leading the New Republic [[Adelphi Rangers|reinforcements]] there.<ref name="Chapter 6" />

For his association with Davan's death and Qin's escape, Mayfeld was sentenced to fifty years at the [[Karthon Chop Fields]],<ref name="Chapter 14">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 14: The Tragedy}}</ref> a New Republic correction facility<ref name="Karthon DB">{{Databank|karthon|Karthon}}</ref> on the prison [[moon]] of [[Karthon]].<ref name="Junior novel 2">[[The Mandalorian Junior Novel|''The Mandalorian'' Junior Novel]]</ref> Djarin's attempt to preserve Davan's life was captured on the correctional transport's security logs. When two [[New Republic pilot]]s intercepted the Mandalorian at<ref name="Chapter 10" /> the [[Water|ice]] [[planet]]<ref name="Maldo Kreis">{{Databank|maldo-kreis|Maldo Kreis}}</ref> [[Maldo Kreis]], the pair pulled up ''Bothan-5''{{'s}} security logs and chose to let Djarin go; one of them, [[Carson Teva]], listed his actions involving Davan as one of the reasons.<ref name="Chapter 10" />

==Personality and traits==
{{Quote|Hey, hey, hey. Listen to me, okay? ''[&hellip;]'' What's your name?''"<br />"''D&mdash; It's Davan.|Din Djarin and Davan|audio=Din-Whats-Your-Name.flac|<ref name="Chapter 6" />}}
Lant Davan was a [[human]] [[Gender|male]] with [[Color|light]] skin, brown [[hair]], and blue [[eye]]s.<ref name="Chapter 6" /> An anxious officer,<ref name="Junior novel" /> he was frightened upon seeing Din Djarin and his team enter the control room, ordering them to drop their blasters in a stuttering manner to no avail. When the team surrounded him,<ref name="Chapter 6" /> the nervous<ref name="PostGCW" /> Davan swiftly revealed a tracking beacon as a threat, breathing heavily as tensions rose. When Djarin attempted to reason with him, asking for his name, Davan stammered in his response.<ref name="Chapter 6" />

==Equipment==
{{Quote|Nice shoes. Matches his belt.|Mayfeld mocks Davan's uniform|audio=Mayfeld-Nice-Shoes.flac|<ref name="Chapter 6" />}}
[[File:Davan_standoff.png|thumb|left|250px|Davan draws his A-180 blaster.]]
Davan wore a blue [[Clothing|uniform]] with a [[Sigil of the New Republic|New Republic insignia]] on the left shoulder. He wore white shoes<ref name="Chapter 6" /> that resembled [[Stormtrooper armor|those]] of an [[Galactic Empire|Imperial]] [[stormtrooper]].<ref name="ANH">{{Film|IV}}</ref> Mayfeld sarcastically remarked that the shoes matched Davan's [[belt]], which displayed an [[officer's disk]] upon the buckle. Davan also wore a white [[helmet]] with a black tinted visor<ref name="Chapter 6" />&mdash;the same type as the ones used by the [[Alderaanian consular security]].<ref name="Rogue One UVG">''[[Star Wars: Rogue One: The Ultimate Visual Guide]]''</ref> Davan's designation, "DC1," was printed in [[Aurebesh]] on the right side of the helmet. He possessed both a [[holster]]ed A-180 blaster and a tracking beacon.<ref name="Chapter 6" />

==Behind the scenes==
===Appearance and conception===
{{Quote|It was secretive, very secretive, as all ''Star Wars'' things are, and I've known that, but even more so in the live action.|Matt Lanter|audio=LanterDavan1.flac|<ref name="SYFY">{{WebCite|url=https://www.syfy.com/syfywire/star-wars-clone-wars-matt-lanter-anakin-skywalker-interview|text=Matt Lanter on the joy of giving ''Star Wars: The Clone Wars'' back to fans, Padmé's pregnancy, and more|author=Silliman, Brian|date=2020-03-07|work=[https://www.syfy.com SYFY WIRE]|archiveurl=https://archive.is/X5tT9}}</ref>}}
[[File:Lant-Davan-concept-art.png|thumb|right|150px|Concept art of Lant Davan by Brian Matyas and Doug Chiang]]
Lant Davan first appeared in "[[Chapter 6: The Prisoner]],"<ref name="Chapter 6" /> the sixth episode of the [[The Mandalorian Season One|first season]] of the live-action television series ''[[Star Wars: The Mandalorian]]''. The episode aired on [[December 13]], [[2019]] on [[Disney+]].<ref name="Insider201">{{InsiderCite|201|Star Wars: The Mandalorian Season One Companion|''Star Wars: The Mandalorian'' Season One Companion}}</ref> Credited as "New Republic soldier" in the episode's end credits, the character was portrayed by [[Matt Lanter]],<ref name="Chapter 6" /> who had previously voiced the character [[Anakin Skywalker]] in various ''[[Star Wars]]'' media such as ''[[Star Wars: The Clone Wars]]'',<ref name="Lanter interview">{{StarWarsShow|blmN_lMJAHk|The Mandalorian Wraps and Matt Lanter Talks The Clone Wars|sw_url=video/the-mandalorian-wraps-and-matt-lanter-talks-the-clone-wars}}</ref> as a small cameo for the episode.<ref name="Companion" /> In the episode, when Djarin and Burg fight in the control room, Davan's body is positioned differently to how it had fallen in his death scene, having moved to the corner of the room.<ref name="Chapter 6" /> The [[2021]] reference book ''[[Star Wars: The Mandalorian Handbook]]'' by [[Matt Jones]] introduced Davan's first name, Lant,<ref name="Handbook" /> made up of the first four letters of Matt Lanter's last name.<ref name="Chapter 6" />

Davan was conceived by artists [[Brian Matyas]] and [[Doug Chiang]], who intended to represent a "cleaner and more romanticized" version of the Alderaanian Fleet Trooper from<ref name="AoTM">[[The Art of Star Wars: The Mandalorian (Season One)|''The Art of Star Wars: The Mandalorian'' Season One]]</ref> the [[1977]] [[original trilogy]] film, [[Star Wars: Episode IV A New Hope|''Star Wars'': Episode IV ''A New Hope'']],<ref name="ANH" /> as a way of showing the progression of the troopers after a few years of the New Republic being in power. The art used was titled "New Republic police version 2C."<ref name="AoTM" />

===Lanter's portrayal===
{{Quote|Every time I got out of my trailer, I had to have a cloak; I was wearing a cloak so no one could see my uniform. It was kind of the first time we've seen that New Republic soldier on film.|Matt Lanter|audio=LanterDavan2.flac|<ref name="SYFY" />}}
[[File:Directing-TMS1C6.png|thumb|left|300px|Matt Lanter on set as Davan]]
According to Lanter, he was asked to play Davan by former ''The Clone Wars'' director [[Dave Filoni]],<ref name="SYFY" /> the executive producer of ''The Mandalorian'',<ref name="Mando photos">{{SW|url=news/swcc-2019-see-new-photos-from-the-mandalorian|text=SWCC 2019: See New Photos from ''The Mandalorian''}}</ref> through the actor's agent.<ref name="Insider 201">{{InsiderCite|201|Matt Lanter: The Animated Anakin}}</ref> The filming for "Chapter 6: The Prisoner," including Davan's scene, took place from [[January 25]] to [[February 2]] of 2019.<ref name="AoTM" /> Lanter expressed enjoyment in taking part in the series, comparing it to working on a ''Star Wars'' film. He also stated that the costume for Davan had to be remade after Filoni realized it was a "slight wrong color" and that because of the secretive nature of the episode's production, Lanter had to wear a cloak over his costume when leaving his trailer to go on set.<ref name="SYFY" />

Lanter said that he had a body scan of himself as Davan in a trailer, where he had photos taken from numerous angles of him in a number of different poses using a large number of cameras. The scan came as a part of what he thought was protocol for all characters, and he speculated that it could be for an action figure. Lanter also explained that during the first take of Davan's death, he stumbled into a fake control panel, which he collided with in the scene, and broke it. This angered the set dresser, who advised Lanter to be more cautious with the set in the next few takes.<ref name="RFR">{{WebCite|url=http://www.rebelforceradio.com/shows/2020/5/1/matt-lanter-amp-last-call-for-anakin-skywalker|text=Matt Lanter & Last Call for Anakin Skywalker|date=2020-05-01|work=[http://www.rebelforceradio.com Rebel Force Radio]|archivedate=20200520082040}}</ref> Additionally, Lanter stated that the moment where Migs Mayfeld mocked Davan's shoes was entirely improvised by Mayfeld's actor, [[Bill Burr]].<ref name="Insider 201" />

===Inconsistencies===
In [[Joe Schreiber]]'s 2021 [[The Mandalorian Junior Novel|junior novelization]] of ''The Mandalorian'', Davan is described to have been knocked out by Xi'an's knife throw.<ref name="Junior novel" /> Contrary to the novel, the card "An Unexpected Complication" from the ''[[2020 Topps Star Wars: The Mandalorian Season 1]]'' trading card set states that Xi'an killed the corrections officer.<ref name="Topps">{{Topps|set=2020 Topps Star Wars: The Mandalorian Season 1|cardname=An Unexpected Complication|link=https://www.tcdb.com/ViewCard.cfm/sid/233698/cid/15449426/2020-Topps-Star-Wars:-The-Mandalorian-Season-1-73-An-Unexpected-Complication}}</ref> Additionally, in "[[Chapter 14: The Tragedy]]" of ''The Mandalorian''{{'s}} [[The Mandalorian Season Two|second season]],<ref name="Chapter 14" /> which aired on [[December 4]], [[2020]],<ref name="D23">{{D23|url=everything-new-you-can-stream-on-disney-in-december-2020|text=Everything New You Can Stream on Disney+ in December 2020}}</ref> Davan's death is listed among the crimes of Migs Mayfeld.<ref name="Chapter 14" /> This article uses the information presented in the latter two sources.

